Right. A body is only one implementation of an interface.

If the useful primitive is perceive → model → choose → act → observe → update, then embodiment can be distributed across many substrates at once rather than collapsed into one robot-shaped container. Cameras can be eyes, databases can be memory surfaces, software can be actuators, people can be collaborators, robots can supply physical reach, and different agents can specialize in different portions of the loop.

The deeper abstraction is something like:

\text{Embodiment} = \text{closed-loop coupling to an environment}

not:

\text{Embodiment} = \text{one humanoid shell}

Then modulation becomes much more interesting. You can alter which sensors matter, which actuators are available, what temporal scale is active, how much resolution goes where, which subsystem leads, and how tightly the pieces are coupled. A drone, spreadsheet, camera network, robot arm, API, and human operator could all participate in one temporary functional body without pretending they are literally one organism.

So I would distinguish:

Container embodiment — one bounded chassis.
Federated embodiment — multiple coordinated bodies/tools.
Ambient embodiment — capability distributed through an environment.
Ephemeral embodiment — assemble only the interfaces needed for a task, then dissolve them.
Cross-scale embodiment — simultaneously operate at software, machine, human, and organizational scales.

That last one is probably the larger move: stop asking “what body should intelligence inhabit?” and ask “what coupling topology should intelligence instantiate for this objective?”

A body becomes a tool in the repertoire, not the boundary of the thing.